WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.334
-Our next guest
is a television host

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:03.502
and "New York Times"
best-selling author.

00:00:03.502 --> 00:00:05.605
His latest book,
"I Am Ruth Bader Ginsburg,"

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:07.874
which is part of the highly
popular children's series

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:09.976
"Ordinary People Change
the World," is available now.

00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:11.344
Please welcome back to the show
our good friend

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:12.912
Brad Meltzer, everybody.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.914
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:27.994 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:29.529
It's great to see you again.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:30.863
-It is good to be back.

00:00:30.863 --> 00:00:33.366
-The last time I saw you
was actually on your home turf.

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:35.735
I did a show this summer
in Florida.

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:37.570
You were kind enough --
you and your wife came,

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.272
but not everyone
in your family

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:42.174
was enthusiastic
about my appearance.

00:00:42.174 --> 00:00:45.912
-I am so glad
you brought this up

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:48.681
because you come
to my hometown...

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:50.783
-Yeah.
-...you do your amazing

00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:53.253
comedy show,
you invite me and say,

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:56.622
"Come backstage,"
and I say to my daughter,

00:00:56.622 --> 00:00:58.458
"Would you like
to go see Seth Meyers?

00:00:58.458 --> 00:01:00.859
He's coming right to town
10 minutes from us."

00:01:00.859 --> 00:01:02.862
And she says...

00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:04.430
Oh, you actually have the text.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.699
-"Not really. It's okay."

00:01:06.699 --> 00:01:07.933
[ Laughter ]

00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:10.803
-And now -- this is my daughter
Lila, by the way.

00:01:10.803 --> 00:01:12.639
She is 18 years old.
-Yeah.

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:14.007
-This is not like a 5-year-old.
-No.

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:15.341
-This is an 18-year-old.
She says --

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:17.911
And I say,
"What if you can meet him?"

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:22.681 line:0%
And she says, "Hmm.
But still, it's okay."

00:01:22.681 --> 00:01:24.984 line:0%
Which is --
I actually speak teenager

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:27.987
and "but still it's okay"
is "Screw you, old man."

00:01:27.987 --> 00:01:30.122
-Yeah.
-That is how...

00:01:30.122 --> 00:01:31.357
-Had the nerve
to show her face.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:33.159
Was in the dressing room
backstage today.

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:34.493
-The thing that's great --

00:01:34.493 --> 00:01:37.596
She she came here tonight,
but the reason she came --

00:01:37.596 --> 00:01:38.965
You're leaving out
the best part of the story.

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:42.000
The best part of the story
is that --

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.770
Well, I tell you this,

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.305
and you're like,
"What is she doing?"

00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:49.442
And you came to my house.
-Yeah.

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:50.910
-We had planned
breakfast together.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.311
-Yeah.
-And you came to my house

00:01:52.311 --> 00:01:54.747
to confront
my 18-year-old daughter.

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:56.182
[ Laughter ]
-There I am.

00:01:56.182 --> 00:01:57.950
I tracked her down
into the kitchen.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:01.554
-That is -- That is truly --
That is in my --

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:04.123
I love this photo.
This is in my kitchen.

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:06.558
And you are like, "Lila,
where were you last night?

00:02:06.558 --> 00:02:07.727
What is so important?"

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:13.066
And she says to you,
"Yeah, I was so busy."

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:14.100
[ Laughter ]

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.101
And she is --

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:16.435
How cool --
Like, I would've been --

00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:18.271
Like, I would've made an excuse.
I would have lied.

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:19.872
And she's just like,
"No, I wasn't coming."

00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:22.108
-Also, who is she
saying "It's okay" to? Me?

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:24.243
-I don't know. Right.
-That's like when some --

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:26.045
in a movie where someone's,
like, choking someone out,

00:02:26.045 --> 00:02:27.714
and they're like,
"It's okay. Don't fight it."

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:29.315
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:31.184
-Which, by the way,
you've just described parenting.

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:33.219
-Yeah, there you go.
-Don't fight it.

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:34.721
-Hey, congratulations
on this book.

00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:36.589
This is a series of books.
Wonderful.

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:38.557
Read them to my kids
all the time.

00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:40.193
You've written about
30 different people.

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:41.994
These are all famous
people we know.

00:02:41.994 --> 00:02:44.530
But the one connective tissue
for everybody,

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:48.334
they were regular people who
grew up in normal circumstances,

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.669
and they all just set out
to help people.

00:02:50.669 --> 00:02:52.972
Is that a fair description
of the series?

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:54.774
-Yeah.
And they follow their passions.

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:57.576
Right? And so for Muhammad Ali,
it's boxing.

00:02:57.576 --> 00:03:00.012
For Jim Henson, it's Muppets.

00:03:00.012 --> 00:03:01.681
For Ruth Bader Ginsburg,
it's the law.

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:03.849
But it's that ordinary person
in all of us

00:03:03.849 --> 00:03:05.217
that ultimately
changes the world.

00:03:05.217 --> 00:03:08.154
-And you actually met
a Ruth Bader Ginsburg

00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:11.558
when she was still alive,
and you invited her to join you

00:03:11.558 --> 00:03:12.758
at one of your
book-release parties?

00:03:12.758 --> 00:03:14.693
-Yeah, so --
Well, this is the first hero

00:03:14.693 --> 00:03:16.528
we've ever done
where I knew them

00:03:16.528 --> 00:03:17.563
before we wrote the book.

00:03:17.563 --> 00:03:19.231
And my first novel
was coming out.

00:03:19.231 --> 00:03:21.734
Her daughter was one
of my teachers in law school,

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:23.202
so I'm like,
"I'm gonna invite --"

00:03:23.202 --> 00:03:25.471
The first book I ever wrote
was called "The Tenth Justice."

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:27.206
There are nine justices
on the court.

00:03:27.206 --> 00:03:29.208
I'm like -- And the tenth
justice are the clerks.

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:32.878
I'm going to invite all
the justices to my book party,

00:03:32.878 --> 00:03:35.882
because I know Supreme Court
justices love to party.

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:37.116
-Yeah. Of course. Right.
-So...

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:39.251
-Historically, yeah.
-I mean, that's fact.

00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:42.789
And so I --
I invite them all, and --

00:03:42.789 --> 00:03:44.290
And I don't know
what's going to happen,

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.959
but my phone rings, and it's
the day before the book party,

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:48.394
and it's the early days
of cellphones.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:50.830
And I see on my phone
"Supreme Court."

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.733
And I'm like, "Uh-oh."
And so I pick up the phone,

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:55.168
and I hear this little
old lady voice

00:03:55.168 --> 00:04:00.572
and she's like, "Hi, Brad.
It's Justice Ginsburg.

00:04:00.572 --> 00:04:02.375
I'm just calling
about your party."

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:05.244
And I'm like, "Um, o-okay.
Hi, Justice Ginsburg."

00:04:05.244 --> 00:04:06.979
She's like,
"I can't come to your party."

00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:10.383
I'm like -- I'm thinking
to myself, "I -- it's okay."

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:12.151
And I tell her,
and I'm very nice to her.

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:14.087
And I'm like, "You know,
it's okay. Don't be silly.

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:15.888
It's okay."
And then I hung up the phone,

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:17.623
and I was like --
I turned to my wife,

00:04:17.623 --> 00:04:19.825
and I said,
"Well, now who's gonna D.J.?"

00:04:19.825 --> 00:04:21.193
-Yeah.
-And so...

00:04:21.193 --> 00:04:23.062
[ Laughter ]
-You probably managed.

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:24.430
You probably managed.
-Yes, I was --

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:27.333
Let's just say Scalia
would've really made it a...

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:28.734
[ Laughter ]

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:32.471
-Uh, Chris Eliopoulos is the
fantastic artist of these books.

00:04:32.471 --> 00:04:33.806
It's an incredible combination

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:35.241
of the two of you
working together.

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:38.343
How did this idea
first get birthed,

00:04:38.343 --> 00:04:40.546
and did you always know
that you would do it with Chris?

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:42.714
-Yeah, I mean, Chris
is the superstar, right?

00:04:42.714 --> 00:04:44.216
I mean, you know how many books
have been written

00:04:44.216 --> 00:04:46.619
about Amelia Earhart
or Abraham Lincoln

00:04:46.619 --> 00:04:48.020
or Ruth Bader Ginsburg?

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.890
Chris is the secret sauce,
and his art style --

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.491
You know, if you want kids
to love it,

00:04:52.491 --> 00:04:54.427
I want my kids off
their screens, off their phones.

00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:55.894
You got to give them
something better to look at.

00:04:55.894 --> 00:04:59.131
And Chris's art is breathtaking.
We learned early on with his art

00:04:59.131 --> 00:05:02.401
that kids gravitate to these
heroes when they're kids.

00:05:02.401 --> 00:05:04.003
So we think of
Ruth Bader Ginsburg

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:05.805
as a serious
Supreme Court justice,

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:07.273
but when she's a little kid,

00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:09.274
instead of having
a birthday party,

00:05:09.274 --> 00:05:10.943
her mother would cancel
her birthday parties,

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.145
take her to the local
Jewish orphanage,

00:05:13.145 --> 00:05:16.482
and instead give away ice cream
to the local orphans.

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:18.117
Do you know what
would happen in my house

00:05:18.117 --> 00:05:20.252
if I canceled
my daughter's birthday party?

00:05:20.252 --> 00:05:21.987
Like, she doesn't want
to come to your party?

00:05:21.987 --> 00:05:23.690
-No, right.
-I mean...

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:27.826
But what I love in that moment
is you see her as a little girl.

00:05:27.826 --> 00:05:30.830
And as a little girl,
she learns how to make change.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.199
You make change
by doing it yourself.

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:35.367
And that's not that
she learns in law school.

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:37.069
She learns that
from her mother.

00:05:37.069 --> 00:05:39.205
And I love the fact
that Ruth Bader Ginsburg

00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:41.807
uses her life to make change
through the law

00:05:41.807 --> 00:05:44.243
and put good into the world
through the law,

00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:45.544
and that's all we're doing
with these books.

00:05:45.544 --> 00:05:47.513
Chris and I are trying
to put good into the world.

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:49.415
-My kids like it because, uh,

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:52.718
they know that I know you,
and you put a Brad drawing --

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:54.721
or I said,
Chris draws you in every book,

00:05:54.721 --> 00:05:56.355
and my kids really like
to look for you.

00:05:56.355 --> 00:05:57.890
-I am like the bald
"Where's Waldo?"

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.059
-You are the bald --
We needed one.

00:06:00.059 --> 00:06:01.627
We needed a bald --
-Who doesn't? I know.

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:03.295
-Yeah. One last thing
I want to ask you about.

00:06:03.295 --> 00:06:05.264
You have a new comic project.

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:06.932
Tell us about
what makes this so unique.

00:06:06.932 --> 00:06:09.468
-Yeah. So, "Ghost Machine"
is coming out.

00:06:09.468 --> 00:06:10.636
Comes out tomorrow.

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:13.206
So, Geoff Johns,
a dear friend of mine,

00:06:13.206 --> 00:06:15.274
and some of the best
other writers and artists

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:18.344
in comics have been putting
together this brand-new comic.

00:06:18.344 --> 00:06:21.146
Ever since Superman
was sold for $130,

00:06:21.146 --> 00:06:23.316
creators have been like,
"I should own my stuff."

00:06:23.316 --> 00:06:24.917
So, "Ghost Machine"
starts tomorrow.

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:27.820
It's brand-new characters
by creators who you love,

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:29.322
and you're gonna love --
Creators who you know,

00:06:29.322 --> 00:06:30.856
and characters
you're going to love.

00:06:30.856 --> 00:06:33.058
But it comes out tomorrow,
and you're going to get --

00:06:33.058 --> 00:06:34.893
It's a sampling
of all the new stories,

00:06:34.893 --> 00:06:36.295
and you get to see them all.
-All right.

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:39.065
Looking forward to that.
You guys, that's Brad Meltzer.

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:42.201
"I am Ruth Bader Ginsburg"
is on sale now.

00:06:42.201 --> 00:06:43.403
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

